## opencode specifics

- **Skills and agents** — the rolepod skills install at
  `~/.config/opencode/skills/<name>/` and auto-trigger from their
  `description:`; 15 specialists install at `~/.config/opencode/agents/*.md`
  and run by description or `@<name>`, each carrying its own protocol.
- **Tier classes** — map cheap / balanced / strong ONCE per session onto the
  models the user has opted into (configured providers), never the whole
  aggregator catalog; a rung costlier than anything configured is a cost
  decision to surface. Cheap = small / fast, balanced = mid flagship,
  strong = the set's top reasoning model; the ONE judge or security
  reviewer runs strong, never the whole fan-out. Cannot classify →
  balanced, and say so.
- **workflow.mode** — Workflow modes: every mode runs the hooks. Commit of private docs, a sub-agent commit or push, a cannot-wait call, a same-file edit by a live sibling session and an untiered or strong fan-out are blocked in every mode; Standard and Full also block an R4 commit without `security-engineer` and an out-of-scope sub-agent write; Full alone blocks untested commits. Everything else warns. OpenCode's plugin applies child ship-operation rules through its supported before-tool hook and session lookup. The fixture proves adapter behavior; it does not prove live enforcement in every installed runtime. Lead commits retain the existing precommit conditions. Review / test evidence gating is Claude-only; every other rule is skill-enforced.
